here's a curious statistic the pandemic  has been good yes good for  time asleep this chart shows electricity  usage  by hour across the day it's collected by  energy supplier bulb  using their network of tens of thousands  of smart meters which they've  exclusively shared with us  now the dark blue line is the last week  of january 2020  before the pandemic the lightning line  is the last week of january 2021 so  during the pandemic  and you can see back before the pandemic  electricity usage  picked in the morning around 7 30.  now it doesn't get going really until  about nine o'clock and the daytime peak  is around lunch there are lots of  individual variations of course  but overall the uk is having a lion  the one upside of the pandemic now you  might be wondering  why i'm telling you this well it's  because we can use this data  to see whether people are staying in  during lockdown  this chart shows all the households who  use more energy at lunch time  than they used in the morning just as  you would if you were going out to work  you can see during the first lockdown  that number fell very rapidly  then it rose again over the summer as  the prime minister urged people to go  back to work  the second lockdown honestly didn't have  much of an impact  and the third lockdown is somewhere  between lockdown one and two  but and this is the important bit look  over here  the number of households going out to  work is going  up since the start of lockdown  by this measure and it's just one  measure lockdown is  losing its grip on the working  population  now you might wonder is this kind of  going out  as measured by your electricity usage is  that correlated with a rise in cases  well this chart suggests that maybe it  is this is a chart  of people who go out to work in london  versus the rest of the uk  london's in light blue and you can see  far fewer people in london  go out to work now the crucial period  is this period in september and  in august and september epidemiologists  were really puzzled  why cases were rising in the rest of the  country but not in london the most  densely packed city in the uk well  here's the answer well here's the answer  we think  far fewer people were going out to work  in london  in this period than the rest of the uk  now of course this measure isn't perfect  we all know that london has been here  really hard in both the first wave  and the second wave with the new variant  but there is some  evidence to suggest that in london  there is some perhaps additional  protection because there are far  many more service jobs than people who  can stay at home  now back to the present you might say  well  hang on if lockdown is weaker then why  are cases in hospitals going and  hospital emissions  going down and the answer is honestly  that we don't really know  but here's one thing that is interesting  the office for national statistics  survey which is normally considered our  most reliable measure of the state of  the pandemic  that shows that cases aren't falling  very fast  in fact they're decreasing quite  gradually and they're still  very high now why that discrepancy  i've spoken to government scientists  about this and they say that one  possible reason  could be that younger people aren't  getting tested  and the data we've seen today suggest  that might be  because they're going out to work it's  just a theory  but the government will be worried by  data like this  because even though the news about the  vaccine is good the risk of new variants  is high they want everyone to keep on  lying in  a bit longer
 

